// heartsim patient case: a pacemaker that stops working. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/case.rs.
Case(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "case_pacemaker_malfunction",
    title: "Dizzy weeks after a pacemaker",
    summary: "A woman who depends on her pacemaker becomes dizzy. Tell a working device from a failing one, and learn the three ways a pacing spike can go wrong.",
    patient: "79-year-old woman, pacemaker fitted six weeks ago for complete heart block, new dizziness and one near-faint at the kitchen table.",
    references: [
        "Cacciapuoti F et al. When pacing fails after generator replacement: a stepwise diagnostic approach to a reversible lead-header interface problem. Reports (MDPI) 2026;9(2):137. doi:10.3390/reports9020137 (open access, CC BY 4.0; used for the clinical pattern only: syncope, complete heart block with a slow escape, loss of capture, a reversible lead problem. The paper does not describe the pacing spikes; their appearance here comes from the simulator's scenario descriptions).",
    ],
    stages: [
        Stage(
            title: "A working pacemaker",
            markdown: r#"
**Mrs Vasquez** has complete heart block, so her ventricles beat only when her pacemaker tells them
to. At the clinic you first look at a normal tracing of her device: a single-chamber ventricular
pacemaker (VVI) set to 60 beats per minute. Each beat starts with a thin vertical **pacing spike**.
The small P waves of her own sinus rhythm march along at a different rate.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_vvi")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What does this tracing show?",
                    choices: [
                        "A ventricular escape rhythm with no device",
                        "Ventricular pacing at 60 per minute, each spike followed by a wide QRS, with P waves unrelated to the QRS",
                        "Left bundle-branch block with a P wave before every QRS",
                        "Atrial fibrillation",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "Every QRS has a spike in front of it and a wide shape, and the P waves have nothing to do with the QRS complexes: the atria are not sensed or paced in this mode, so the chambers beat independently.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Why are the paced QRS complexes wide?",
                    choices: [
                        "The pacing spike itself is wide",
                        "The patient has low potassium",
                        "The lead sits at the right ventricular apex, so the ventricles are activated slowly from the bottom up, like a bundle-branch block",
                        "The device is failing",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "A paced beat spreads from muscle to muscle from the tip of the lead, not along the fast conduction system. That gives a wide QRS that looks like left bundle-branch block, with a T wave pointing the other way.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "The dizzy spell",
            markdown: r#"
Today the ECG at the time of her dizziness looks different. For the first few seconds it looks as
before, then the spikes carry on but **most of them are not followed by a QRS complex**. The only
beats are a few wide, slow beats at about 30 per minute.

(The simulator loads a device whose threshold, the energy needed to make the heart muscle respond,
rises past the device's output after about eight seconds.)
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_loss_of_capture")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(14000.0))),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What is the problem?",
                    choices: [
                        "Undersensing: the device does not see her own beats",
                        "Oversensing: the device sees signals that are not beats",
                        "A normal paced rhythm",
                        "Failure to capture: spikes are delivered but the ventricle does not respond",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "The spikes are on time, but there is no QRS after most of them, although the muscle is not refractory. The device is discharging but the heart is not responding.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Why is this especially dangerous for Mrs Vasquez?",
                    choices: [
                        "She depends on the pacemaker, so with no capture only a slow escape rhythm remains, and that can cause dizziness or fainting",
                        "Any failure to capture causes ventricular fibrillation",
                        "It makes the sinus node stop",
                        "It is not dangerous because her own heart beats normally",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "With complete heart block her own sinus rhythm cannot reach the ventricles. When pacing fails, the ventricles rely on a slow escape rhythm, here about 30 per minute, which may be too slow to maintain the circulation.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which of these can cause loss of capture?",
                    choices: [
                        "A battery that is too full",
                        "Her sinus rate being too fast",
                        "A rise of the capture threshold at the lead tip, a lead that has moved or is not seated properly, or a metabolic disturbance such as high potassium",
                        "Sleeping on the left side",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "The threshold can rise as scar forms around the lead tip in the weeks after implantation, and a lead that has shifted or is poorly seated in the generator gives a similar picture. The case report this page draws on describes syncope after a device procedure caused by a reversible lead problem. Devices are interrogated to measure the threshold and the lead impedance.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Spikes where they do not belong",
            markdown: r#"
A different patient's device has a different fault. His own heart works (sinus rhythm at 75 with a
normal QRS) but the pacing spikes come at **a fixed rate of their own, whatever his beats are doing**.
Some are followed by a wide paced beat, some land just after his own narrow QRS and are followed by
nothing, and some land on the T wave.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_undersensing")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What kind of fault is this?",
                    choices: [
                        "Undersensing: the device does not see his own beats, so it paces regardless and competes with them",
                        "Failure to capture",
                        "Oversensing of the T wave",
                        "A normal device in a patient with a fast rhythm",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "A device that fails to see intrinsic beats cannot be inhibited by them, so it paces at the programmed rate. Causes include a sensitivity setting that is too insensitive and a small signal at the lead tip.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "A spike falls just after one of his own QRS complexes and is followed by no beat. Is that loss of capture?",
                    choices: [
                        "Yes, the threshold has risen",
                        "Yes, the lead has fractured",
                        "No, the battery is empty",
                        "No: the muscle is still refractory from his own beat, so a spike cannot capture it",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "A spike delivered while the ventricle is refractory cannot make it respond. This is functional non-capture, and it does not mean the lead or the threshold is at fault. A spike on a T wave is a different worry: it can start a dangerous arrhythmia.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Too few spikes",
            markdown: r#"
A third patient has complete heart block and a device set to 60. His tracing shows a **spike
about every 1.3 seconds**, around 46 per minute, with a wide paced QRS after each spike. Nothing is
missing after the spikes, but they are too far apart.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_t_wave_oversensing")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "The device is set to 60 but paces at about 46. What is the most likely explanation?",
                    choices: [
                        "The device battery is nearly empty",
                        "Oversensing: the device counts each paced T wave as a beat and restarts its timer",
                        "Loss of capture",
                        "His sinus node is slowing",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "If the device is set to be very sensitive, it treats the T wave as a heartbeat and waits another full interval before pacing. The spikes are spaced longer than programmed, and each is followed by a QRS.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "After the device check",
            markdown: r#"
Mrs Vasquez's device is interrogated and the problem is found and corrected. (The simulator has no
way to reprogram a device during a case, so this is told, not shown.) The simulator now loads a
**dual-chamber pacemaker (DDD)** for a normal tracing: each sinus P wave is sensed and a
spike follows it after a set delay, and each spike is followed by a QRS.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_ddd")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which finding on the tracing shows that capture is restored?",
                    choices: [
                        "The spikes are thin",
                        "The heart rate is below 60",
                        "Every spike is followed by a QRS complex, and the paced rate follows the programmed rhythm",
                        "There are no P waves",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "A spike followed by a depolarisation on every beat is capture. In this mode the device also follows her P waves, so the atria and ventricles beat in step again.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
